{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792851462,"nonce":"7ceCEG+mMxQq7IaEaHaVCFbhhN4=","title":"Reload notice before a module's new body runs, and a program's subscription","message":"ChimpMonk keeps what a guest module registered with the engine (systems, listeners) and has to drop it before the module's new body runs and registers again. Before this, the world raised `Event::Reload` only after a reload. For Lua and Python the new body had already run by then. The subscription was also Rust-only (`World::on`).\n\nAgreed with ChimpMonk (5622c4b there):\n- `Event::Reloading` is raised once the new source is known to compile, immediately before any of it runs. `Adapter::reload` gets a `ready` callback to call at that point.\n- A source that does not compile raises no `Reloading`, only `Reload` with its error. The old module stays live with its registrations.\n- `core:Modules.onReload(handler)` (`core.Modules` in Haxe) calls `handler(namespace, module, before, error)`.","files":null}]}